{"meta": {"course": "ai-ethics", "chapter": "02", "section": "02", "chapter_title": "Bias and Fairness", "title": "Module 2: Fairness Definitions and Their Impossibilities", "description": "Compare demographic parity, equalized odds, calibration within groups, individual fairness, and counterfactual fairness, and reason about the impossibility theorem that links them when base rates differ.", "learning_objectives": ["State demographic parity, equalized odds, and calibration within groups in plain language and in formal notation.", "Explain the Chouldechova / Kleinberg-Mullainathan-Raghavan impossibility result for unequal base rates.", "Match a fairness criterion to a deployment context based on which kind of error causes the larger concrete harm.", "Document a defensible fairness choice including the criteria not satisfied and the rationale."], "concept_ids": ["fairness_definitions", "demographic_parity", "equalized_odds", "calibration_within_groups", "fairness_impossibility", "individual_fairness", "counterfactual_fairness"], "prereq_section_ids": ["ai-ethics/02/01"], "practice_ids": ["fairness_impossibility", "fairness_demographic_parity", "fairness_match_to_harm", "fairness_individual_metric"], "estimated_time_minutes": 15, "order": 2}, "content": "# Fairness Definitions and Their Impossibilities\n\n> **\"Sometimes the best way to make a system fairer is to admit which fairness you are choosing, and which you are giving up.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Opening: The COMPAS Controversy\n\nIn May 2016, the newsroom ProPublica published \"Machine Bias\" (Angwin, Larson, Mattu, and Kirchner), an investigation of COMPAS, a recidivism risk tool used by U.S. courts to inform bail and sentencing decisions. ProPublica reported that among defendants who did not go on to reoffend, Black defendants were labeled high-risk almost twice as often as white defendants (a false-positive rate of roughly 45% versus 23%). The company behind COMPAS, Northpointe (now Equivant), replied that its tool was in fact fair, because a given risk score predicted the same probability of reoffending regardless of race. The tool was calibrated.\n\nHere is the surprising part: both sides were right about their own numbers. ProPublica was measuring equality of error rates. Northpointe was measuring calibration. The public debate looked like a factual dispute, but it was really a dispute over which definition of fairness should govern the tool. Within a year, researchers proved that, given the conditions present in the data, no model could satisfy both definitions at once.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** A request for \"a fair model\" is under-specified. The fairness literature contains at least twenty formally defined criteria, and the central technical result of the field, proved by Chouldechova (2017) and independently by Kleinberg, Mullainathan, and Raghavan (2016), is that the most natural fairness definitions are mutually incompatible. The engineer's job is to surface the trade-off, choose deliberately, and document the choice, rather than silently pick one definition and hide the rest.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this module, you will be able to:\n\n1. **State** demographic parity, equalized odds, and calibration within groups in both plain language and formal notation.\n2. **Explain** the Chouldechova / Kleinberg-Mullainathan-Raghavan impossibility result for unequal base rates, and demonstrate it on concrete numbers.\n3. **Match** a fairness criterion to a deployment context based on which kind of error causes the larger concrete harm.\n4. **Document** a defensible fairness choice, including the criteria you are not satisfying and the rationale.\n\n---\n\n## Why \"Fair\" Is a Family of Definitions, Not One\n\nThis module covers the three most-used fairness criteria, the formal impossibility result that links them, and the practical workflow for choosing among them. The hard truth from the COMPAS case generalizes: when a stakeholder asks for a \"fair\" model, they have not yet told you which fairness they mean, and an engineer who picks one quietly has still made an ethical decision, just an undocumented one.\n\n---\n\n## The Three Workhorse Definitions\n\nConsider a binary classifier predicting whether a person should receive a beneficial outcome (a loan approved, a job interview, a low-risk diagnosis). Let A be a protected group attribute (e.g., race, gender). The three most-cited group-level fairness criteria are:\n\n**Demographic parity** (also called statistical parity). Selection rate is the same across groups:\n\n$$P(\\hat{Y} = 1 \\mid A = a) = P(\\hat{Y} = 1 \\mid A = b)$$\n\nPlain reading: the model approves the same fraction of each group.\n\n**Equalized odds**. The error rates (false positive rate and false negative rate) are the same across groups:\n\n$$P(\\hat{Y} = 1 \\mid Y = 0, A = a) = P(\\hat{Y} = 1 \\mid Y = 0, A = b)$$\n$$P(\\hat{Y} = 0 \\mid Y = 1, A = a) = P(\\hat{Y} = 0 \\mid Y = 1, A = b)$$\n\nPlain reading: among people who actually qualify, the model misses them at the same rate; among people who do not qualify, the model wrongly approves them at the same rate.\n\n**Calibration within groups**. A predicted score s means the same thing across groups:\n\n$$P(Y = 1 \\mid \\hat{S} = s, A = a) = P(Y = 1 \\mid \\hat{S} = s, A = b)$$\n\nPlain reading: when the model says \"70% chance,\" roughly 70% of those people qualify, regardless of group.\n\nEach definition formalizes a different ethical intuition. Demographic parity asks for **proportional outcomes**. Equalized odds asks for **proportional errors**. Calibration asks for **trust the score equally**.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A vendor says 'a score of 0.7 means a 70% chance of the outcome, equally for every group.' Which fairness criterion is the vendor claiming to satisfy?\" explanation=\"That is the definition of calibration within groups: conditioned on the score, the outcome probability is the same across groups. It says nothing about equal selection rates (demographic parity) or equal error rates (equalized odds), and in fact conflicts with equalized odds when base rates differ.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Demographic parity\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Equalized odds\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Calibration within groups\",\"correct\":true}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## The Impossibility Result\n\nWhen the **base rate** of qualification differs across groups (i.e., the proportion of people who actually qualify is not equal across A = a and A = b), no model can satisfy demographic parity, equalized odds, and calibration simultaneously, except by being a trivial classifier.\n\nChouldechova showed this in the COMPAS context: a recidivism risk model can be calibrated within race groups (a given score predicts the same recidivism rate across groups) **or** equalize false-positive rates across groups (the model is wrong upward at the same rate across groups), but not both, because base rates of recidivism differ in the historical data used.\n\nThis is not a bug to fix with better engineering. It is a mathematical fact about the relationship among the criteria when base rates differ. **You must choose which property to give up.** Engineers who pretend the choice has not been made have made it.\n\nThe cleanest way to see why is an algebraic identity (Chouldechova, 2017) linking the false-positive rate to the base rate $p$, the positive predictive value (PPV), and the false-negative rate (FNR):\n\n$$\\text{FPR} = \\frac{p}{1-p}\\cdot\\frac{1-\\text{PPV}}{\\text{PPV}}\\cdot(1-\\text{FNR})$$\n\nHold PPV and FNR equal across two groups (that is what calibration plus equal recall buys you). The only remaining free term is the base-rate factor $p/(1-p)$. If $p$ differs across groups, the FPRs are forced to differ. There is no remaining degree of freedom to rescue them.\n\nDrive the identity yourself. Fix PPV and FNR (shared across both groups), then sweep the base rate and watch the false-positive rate move on its own.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Chouldechova FPR identity: hold PPV and FNR equal, vary the base rate\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"p\",\"label\":\"Base rate (share who truly qualify)\",\"min\":0.05,\"max\":0.9,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.3},{\"name\":\"PPV\",\"label\":\"Positive predictive value (calibration target)\",\"min\":0.5,\"max\":0.95,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.75},{\"name\":\"FNR\",\"label\":\"False-negative rate (1 - recall)\",\"min\":0.05,\"max\":0.5,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.2}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Forced false-positive rate (FPR)\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"(p / (1 - p)) * ((1 - PPV) / PPV) * (1 - FNR)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"p\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nNotice the move: with PPV and FNR pinned (the calibration-plus-equal-recall package), the FPR is a strictly increasing function of the base rate. Two groups with different base rates therefore cannot share an FPR unless you break one of the equalities you pinned. That is the impossibility, made tactile.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Case: Making the Impossibility Numeric\n\nAbstract impossibility claims are easy to nod along to and hard to feel. Let us build two concrete confusion matrices and watch the contradiction appear.\n\n**Setup.** A lender scores 2,000 applicants for a loan, 1,000 in group A and 1,000 in group B. \"Qualified\" (the positive class $Y=1$) means the applicant would repay. The two groups have different base rates, which is the realistic case after historical disinvestment:\n\n- Group A base rate: $p_A = 0.30$, so 300 of 1,000 truly qualify.\n- Group B base rate: $p_B = 0.60$, so 600 of 1,000 truly qualify.\n\n**Goal.** Build a classifier that is calibration-like (equal PPV across groups, here 0.75) and has equal recall (equal FNR across groups, here 0.20). These two equalities feel fair: a \"yes\" means the same thing in both groups, and the model misses qualified people at the same rate in both groups. Then we will read off the false-positive rates and see what survived.\n\n**Step 1: Group A counts.** With 300 actual positives and FNR = 0.20, false negatives are $0.20 \\times 300 = 60$, so true positives are $300 - 60 = 240$. To get PPV = 0.75, total predicted positives must be $240 / 0.75 = 320$, so false positives are $320 - 240 = 80$. The 700 actual negatives split into 80 false positives and 620 true negatives.\n\n**Step 2: Group B counts.** With 600 actual positives and FNR = 0.20, false negatives are $0.20 \\times 600 = 120$, so true positives are $600 - 120 = 480$. For PPV = 0.75, predicted positives are $480 / 0.75 = 640$, so false positives are $640 - 480 = 160$. The 400 actual negatives split into 160 false positives and 240 true negatives.\n\n**Step 3: Tabulate and compute the false-positive rates.**\n\n| Quantity | Group A | Group B |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Truly qualified ($Y=1$) | 300 | 600 |\n| Truly not qualified ($Y=0$) | 700 | 400 |\n| True positives (TP) | 240 | 480 |\n| False negatives (FN) | 60 | 120 |\n| False positives (FP) | 80 | 160 |\n| True negatives (TN) | 620 | 240 |\n| PPV $= \\text{TP}/(\\text{TP}+\\text{FP})$ | $240/320 = 0.75$ | $480/640 = 0.75$ |\n| FNR $= \\text{FN}/(\\text{TP}+\\text{FN})$ | $60/300 = 0.20$ | $120/600 = 0.20$ |\n| **FPR $= \\text{FP}/(\\text{FP}+\\text{TN})$** | $80/700 = \\boxed{0.114}$ | $160/400 = \\boxed{0.400}$ |\n\n**Step 4: Read the result.** We enforced equal PPV and equal FNR, and we still ended up with a false-positive rate of 11.4% for group A and 40.0% for group B. A group-B applicant who will not repay is more than three times as likely to be wrongly approved (and, in a risk-screening framing, a group-B person who poses no risk is over three times as likely to be wrongly flagged). The algebraic identity predicted exactly this: $\\frac{0.6/0.4}{0.3/0.7} = \\frac{1.5}{0.4286} = 3.5$, and indeed $0.400 / 0.114 = 3.5$. The numbers were not chosen to be adversarial; they fell out of insisting on two reasonable-sounding equalities at once.\n\nThis is the COMPAS dispute in miniature. If you announce \"calibrated within groups\" as your fairness banner, you have silently accepted unequal false-positive exposure. If you instead force equal FPR, you will break calibration and equal recall. The data does not let you have all three.\n\nReconstruct Group B's confusion matrix step by step, predicting each count before it reveals.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Build Group B (base rate 0.60) and read off its FPR\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Group B has 600 truly qualified out of 1,000. With FNR fixed at 0.20, how many qualified people does the model miss (false negatives)?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"60\",\"120\",\"480\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"0.20 x 600 = 120 false negatives, so true positives = 600 - 120 = 480.\"},{\"prompt\":\"To hold PPV at 0.75 with 480 true positives, how many false positives are allowed?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"80\",\"160\",\"240\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Predicted positives = 480 / 0.75 = 640, so false positives = 640 - 480 = 160.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The 400 truly-not-qualified split into 160 FP and 240 TN. What is Group B\u2019s false-positive rate?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"0.114\",\"0.250\",\"0.400\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"FPR = 160 / 400 = 0.400, versus 0.114 for Group A. Same PPV, same FNR, FPR off by a factor of 3.5.\"},{\"reveal\":\"That 3.5x ratio equals (0.6/0.4) / (0.3/0.7) exactly. The base-rate gap, not any modeling sloppiness, forced the false-positive disparity once PPV and FNR were pinned.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n> **Critical Check:** Before debating which definition is right, always compute the base-rate gap in your data. If base rates are (nearly) equal, the criteria can largely agree and there is no forced trade-off. The dilemma is created by the gap, not by the math.\n\n---\n\n## Trade-off Frame: What Are You Willing to Give Up?\n\nEach fairness criterion has a downside when other criteria are not enforced.\n\n**Demographic parity** ignores ground-truth qualification. If 60% of group A qualifies and 30% of group B does, demographic parity may force selecting unqualified members of group B and rejecting qualified members of group A. The trade-off: equality of outcomes at the cost of accuracy on individuals.\n\n**Equalized odds** is sensitive to ground truth Y. When ground truth is itself biased (historical bias from the previous module), equalizing errors against a contaminated label perpetuates the original injustice. The trade-off: depends on how trustworthy the labels are.\n\n**Calibration within groups** preserves predictive validity but allows error rates to differ across groups when base rates differ. The trade-off: equally trustworthy scores at the cost of unequal exposure to false-positive harm.\n\nChoosing among these requires a **stakeholder-specific judgment**: which kind of harm is worse for the deployment context? In hiring, false negatives (qualified people screened out) typically harm the candidate; demographic parity tends to be the louder demand. In criminal risk, false positives (incorrectly classified as high-risk) cause concrete harm; equalized false-positive rates becomes the louder demand.\n\nThe technical fact does not give you the ethical answer. The technical fact tells you which question you are answering when you pick a criterion.\n\nTo make the choice concrete, lay the criteria against the stakeholders who bear each kind of harm. For the lending model above, a decision table makes the tension visible:\n\n| Criterion enforced | Who is protected | Who bears the residual harm | Best fit when |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Demographic parity | Applicants in the lower-base-rate group (equal access) | Lender (more defaults) and qualified applicants displaced | Access to the resource is the primary justice concern and labels are distrusted |\n| Equalized odds / equal FPR | People wrongly flagged or wrongly denied | Predictive accuracy; calibration breaks | A false positive causes concrete, asymmetric harm (e.g., wrongful detention) |\n| Calibration within groups | Decision-makers who must trust scores | The higher-base-rate group's wrongly-approved/flagged share rises | Scores feed many downstream decisions and must mean one thing |\n\nReading the table for COMPAS: a defendant carries the cost of a false positive (loss of liberty), which is why advocates pressed for equal FPR; a judge needs a score that means one thing, which is why the vendor defended calibration. Naming the stakeholder behind each column is how you convert an abstract impossibility into a defensible decision.\n\nMatch each criterion to the harm it best controls.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Equalized odds / equal FPR\",\"definition\":\"A false positive causes concrete asymmetric harm, e.g. wrongful detention in criminal risk\"},{\"term\":\"Demographic parity\",\"definition\":\"Equal access to the resource is the primary justice concern and the labels are distrusted\"},{\"term\":\"Calibration within groups\",\"definition\":\"One score feeds many downstream decisions and must mean the same thing everywhere\"},{\"term\":\"Individual fairness\",\"definition\":\"Near-identical applicants must not receive opposite outcomes regardless of group\"},{\"term\":\"Counterfactual fairness\",\"definition\":\"The decision should be unchanged in a world where only the protected attribute differs\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"fairness-impossibility-trilemma\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## Individual vs. Group Fairness\n\nThe three definitions above are **group fairness**. A different family asks about **individual fairness**: similar individuals should receive similar treatment, regardless of group membership (Dwork et al., 2012). Formally:\n\n$$d(\\hat{Y}(x), \\hat{Y}(x')) \\leq L \\cdot d(x, x')$$\n\nfor some distance metric d on input space and a Lipschitz constant L. Individuals close in input space should have close outcomes.\n\nIndividual fairness is intuitively appealing but defers the entire problem to the choice of distance metric d. Choosing d that excludes A from \"similarity\" makes group fairness implications visible; choosing d that includes A bakes group structure back in. The choice of d is itself an ethical decision.\n\nIn practice, defensible designs often blend a group-fairness criterion (to address structural inequities) with individual fairness within group (to prevent arbitrary individual treatment).\n\n---\n\n## Counterfactual Fairness\n\nKusner, Loftus, Russell, and Silva (2017) proposed a different family: a decision is **counterfactually fair** if it would have been the same in a counterfactual world where the protected attribute A took a different value, holding everything else causally upstream constant.\n\nThis requires a causal model of the data-generating process, itself a strong assumption. When the causal model is defensible, counterfactual fairness aligns the decision with the intuition \"would this person have been treated differently if their race/gender/age were different?\" When the causal model is contested, counterfactual fairness becomes a debate over the model rather than the decision.\n\n---\n\n## Practical Workflow\n\nWhen stakeholders request \"a fair model,\" walk this checklist:\n\n1. **Surface the under-specification.** State explicitly that \"fair\" is a family of definitions, and pick at least two candidate criteria.\n2. **Examine base rates.** Compute the actual base-rate gap across groups in the data. If base rates differ, the impossibility result applies and a choice is unavoidable.\n3. **Match criterion to harm.** Identify which kind of error (false positive vs false negative) causes the larger concrete harm in the deployment context.\n4. **Document the choice.** Write the chosen criterion, the criteria that will not be satisfied, and the deployment context's reasoning. This is the audit trail that survives turnover.\n5. **Monitor.** Subgroup-disaggregated metrics in production. Drift in base rates, error rates, or score distributions should trigger re-evaluation.\n\nThe goal is not to \"achieve fairness.\" The goal is to **make a defensible, documented, stakeholder-aligned choice among incompatible definitions**, and to monitor the choice's consequences over time.\n\nNow you own the choice. A pretrial risk tool, unequal base rates, two camps in the room.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Choosing a fairness criterion for a pretrial risk tool\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Base rates of re-arrest differ across groups in your historical data. Defense advocates demand equal false-positive rates; the court demands a score that means one thing for every defendant. You measure the base-rate gap first. It is large. What do you commit to?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Calibration within groups, and say nothing about the FPR consequence\",\"next\":\"calib_silent\",\"feedback\":\"You picked a defensible criterion but hid its cost. Silence about the forgone criterion is still an ethical choice, just an undocumented one.\"},{\"label\":\"Equal false-positive rate, because wrongful detention is the asymmetric harm here\",\"next\":\"eqfpr\",\"feedback\":\"A liberty-protecting choice. But you must own what it breaks.\"},{\"label\":\"Insist engineering can satisfy both calibration and equal FPR with enough tuning\",\"next\":\"deny\",\"feedback\":\"With unequal base rates this is mathematically impossible, not an engineering gap.\"}]},\"calib_silent\":{\"prompt\":\"Months later ProPublica-style reporting shows your tool flags non-reoffending defendants in one group far more often. The court is blindsided. What was the avoidable error?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Choosing calibration\",\"next\":\"end_calib\",\"feedback\":\"Calibration was defensible; failing to DOCUMENT the forgone equal-FPR and brief stakeholders was the avoidable error.\"},{\"label\":\"Not documenting the forgone criterion and its FPR cost\",\"next\":\"end_doc\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. The deliverable is the documented trade-off, not a silent pick.\"}]},\"eqfpr\":{\"prompt\":\"Equalizing FPR breaks calibration: a given score now predicts different reoffense rates by group, so the judge cannot read scores uniformly. How do you make this defensible?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Document that calibration is forgone, justify by the asymmetric liberty harm, and add subgroup monitoring\",\"next\":\"end_good\",\"feedback\":\"Best practice: deliberate choice, named forgone criterion, harm-based rationale, standing monitor.\"},{\"label\":\"Hide the calibration loss to avoid alarming the court\",\"next\":\"calib_silent\",\"feedback\":\"Concealment recreates the COMPAS controversy from the other side.\"}]},\"deny\":{\"prompt\":\"The team burns a sprint trying and fails. Where does that leave you?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Accept the impossibility and make a documented choice\",\"next\":\"eqfpr\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The theorem sets the shape of the trade-off; it does not excuse pretending there is none.\"}]},\"end_good\":{\"prompt\":\"You shipped a documented, monitored, harm-aligned choice.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the win: surface the under-specification, measure the base-rate gap, match criterion to the dominant harm, document the forgone criterion, and monitor. The audit trail survives team turnover.\",\"choices\":[]},\"end_doc\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson locked.\",\"feedback\":\"The chosen criterion plus the explicitly named forgone criterion and rationale is the actual deliverable.\",\"choices\":[]},\"end_calib\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider.\",\"feedback\":\"Calibration itself was fine; the failure was non-disclosure. Re-run the branch and document the trade-off.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"A colleague says: 'Just tune the model harder and it will be calibrated AND have equal false-positive rates across groups.' Explain why this is impossible when base rates differ, and what the team should do instead.\" answer=\"When the base rate of the positive outcome differs across groups, the Chouldechova / Kleinberg-Mullainathan-Raghavan result proves that calibration within groups and equal false-positive (and false-negative) rates cannot all hold simultaneously except for a trivial classifier. The algebraic identity FPR = [p/(1-p)] x [(1-PPV)/PPV] x (1-FNR) shows that once PPV and FNR are equalized across groups, the FPR is pinned to the base-rate factor p/(1-p); unequal p forces unequal FPR. No amount of tuning removes that term. Instead of chasing the impossible, the team should measure the base-rate gap, decide which error causes the larger concrete harm in this context, pick the matching criterion, and document the criterion deliberately forgone along with the rationale.\"></self-explain>\n\n---\n\n## Counterarguments and Limitations\n\nA careful engineer should hold these definitions loosely. Several legitimate objections temper the framework above.\n\n**\"The base rates themselves are unjust, so equalizing against them launders bias.\"** This is the strongest objection. If group B's lower repayment rate (or higher recidivism rate) reflects historical disinvestment, over-policing, or biased labeling rather than an immutable fact, then any criterion anchored to observed $Y$ inherits that injustice. Calibration and equalized odds both trust the label. When the label is suspect, the more defensible move may be to attack the data-generating process (better labels, causal modeling, or demographic parity as a corrective) rather than optimize against a contaminated ground truth.\n\n**Group fairness can be satisfied while individuals are treated arbitrarily.** A model can hit demographic parity exactly and still hand near-identical applicants opposite outcomes. This is why individual fairness exists as a separate family; the two are not substitutes.\n\n**The protected attribute is rarely clean.** Race, gender, and disability are often missing, self-reported inconsistently, or correlated with dozens of proxies (zip code, name, browsing history). \"Just remove the protected attribute\" (fairness through unawareness) is widely shown to fail, because the model reconstructs the attribute from proxies. Auditing therefore requires the protected attribute to be present, not absent.\n\n**Impossibility is about exact equality, not about improvement.** The theorem forbids satisfying all criteria perfectly and simultaneously when base rates differ. It does not forbid reducing disparities on every axis at once. In practice teams negotiate small, bounded gaps on several metrics rather than insisting one metric be exactly equal. The theorem sets the shape of the trade-off; it does not excuse inaction.\n\n**A fair classifier in a unfair system is still embedded in that system.** Equalizing a loan model does nothing about the wealth gap that produced the base-rate difference. Fairness metrics are a local repair on one decision point, not a theory of justice. Treating a passing fairness audit as proof of an ethical system is a category error.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Definitions at a Glance\n\n| Criterion | Plain reading | What it ignores |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Demographic parity | Equal selection rate across groups | Ground-truth qualification |\n| Equalized odds | Equal FPR and FNR across groups | Validity when labels are biased |\n| Calibration within groups | A score means the same thing across groups | Equal error exposure when base rates differ |\n| Individual fairness | Similar people, similar outcomes | Hides the ethics inside the distance metric $d$ |\n| Counterfactual fairness | Same decision if the attribute were different | Requires a contested causal model |\n\nSee how the five criteria and the impossibility result relate as one family.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"fairness_definitions\",\"demographic_parity\",\"equalized_odds\",\"calibration_within_groups\",\"fairness_impossibility\",\"individual_fairness\",\"counterfactual_fairness\"]'></concept-map>\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- \"Fair\" is a family of definitions, not one; a vague request hides an unmade choice.\n- When base rates differ across groups, calibration, equalized odds, and demographic parity cannot all hold at once (Chouldechova; Kleinberg-Mullainathan-Raghavan). Our lending example produced FPRs of 11.4% versus 40.0% while holding PPV and FNR equal.\n- The technical result tells you which trade-off you face; it does not tell you which side to choose. That is a stakeholder-specific, harm-weighted judgment.\n- Match the criterion to the harm: false-positive harm pushes toward equal FPR; access concerns push toward demographic parity; multi-use scores push toward calibration.\n- Document the criterion chosen, the criteria forgone, and the reasoning. The audit trail is the deliverable that survives team turnover.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nThe next module moves from defining fairness to **measuring and mitigating** it: how to compute subgroup-disaggregated metrics on real data, where in the pipeline (pre-processing, in-processing, post-processing) to intervene, and how each mitigation interacts with the impossibility constraints established here.\n\n---\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Fairness Trade-offs\" context=\"A team is building a college-admissions support model. Historical data shows different graduation rates by race, partly because of historical disinvestment in some schools and partly because of differential support during college. Stakeholders ask for 'a fair model.' Walk the checklist: which fairness criterion best fits this context, what trade-off is the team accepting, and what monitoring should be in place?\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n### Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A risk-assessment model is calibrated within race groups (a given score predicts the same outcome rate across groups). When base rates of the outcome differ across groups, what is the most accurate statement about the false-positive rates across groups?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"They will also be equal automatically; calibration implies equalized odds.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"They cannot be equalized simultaneously with calibration when base rates differ; this is the Chouldechova / Kleinberg-Mullainathan-Raghavan impossibility result. The team must choose which property to give up.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"There is no relationship between the two.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. The impossibility theorem says that with unequal base rates, calibration and equalized error rates cannot both be satisfied. This is a mathematical fact, not an engineering deficit.\"></interactive-quiz>\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "fairness_impossibility", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "fairness_impossibility", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["fairness_impossibility", "calibration_within_groups", "equalized_odds"], "stem": "A risk-assessment model is calibrated within race groups. The base rate of the outcome differs across groups. Can the model also equalize false-positive rates across groups?", "options": ["Yes, calibration implies equalized error rates automatically.", "No. The Chouldechova / Kleinberg-Mullainathan-Raghavan impossibility result shows calibration and equalized error rates cannot both hold when base rates differ. A choice must be made.", "Only if the model uses deep learning.", "Only on weekdays."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The impossibility theorem is a mathematical fact about the relationship between calibration and equalized error rates when base rates differ. Engineering cannot escape it; it can only choose where to spend the trade-off.", "misconception_id": "fairness_criteria_compatible"}, {"id": "fairness_demographic_parity", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "demographic_parity", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["demographic_parity", "fairness_definitions"], "stem": "Demographic parity is best described as:", "options": ["Equal selection rates across protected groups, regardless of ground-truth qualification.", "Equal error rates across groups.", "Equal predicted scores across groups.", "Equal sample sizes per group in training."], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "Demographic parity equates selection rates across groups. It does not look at ground-truth Y; that is its strength (proportional outcomes) and its weakness (ignores qualification).", "misconception_id": "demographic_parity_is_error_equality"}, {"id": "fairness_match_to_harm", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "fairness_definitions", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["fairness_definitions", "equalized_odds"], "stem": "In a criminal-risk context where false positives (incorrect high-risk classification) cause concrete harm to defendants, which fairness criterion is most often the louder requirement and why?", "options": ["Demographic parity, because all groups must be classified the same way.", "Equalized odds (specifically, equal false-positive rates across groups), because the harm of a false positive is borne directly by the misclassified individual and unequal exposure to that harm is the central injustice.", "Calibration within groups; the score should mean the same thing across groups even if false positives differ.", "All criteria are equivalent in this context."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Match the criterion to where the concrete harm lands. In criminal-risk deployment, false-positive harm is direct and severe; equalizing false-positive rates is the louder demand.", "misconception_id": "fairness_is_one_size_fits_all"}, {"id": "fairness_individual_metric", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "individual_fairness", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["individual_fairness", "counterfactual_fairness"], "stem": "Individual fairness ('similar individuals should be treated similarly') has a key under-specification. What is it?", "options": ["It requires deep learning.", "It defers the entire ethical problem to the choice of distance metric d that defines 'similar' \u2014 and the choice of d is itself an ethical decision.", "It is mathematically impossible.", "It only applies to small datasets."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Individual fairness's content depends on what 'similar' means. Picking d to exclude or include the protected attribute encodes group-fairness implications back into the criterion.", "misconception_id": "individual_fairness_is_objective"}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "0e6486ef2683788c91117b212d07d683c2dc195382ab05152f45af7ec75683eb", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "fairness_criteria_compatible", "pattern": "ignores_impossibility", "description": "Believing that demographic parity, equalized odds, and calibration can all be satisfied simultaneously", "trigger": "learner promises a model that is 'fair on every metric'", "feedback": "When base rates differ, the impossibility theorem says these criteria cannot all hold at once. A defensible design surfaces the trade-off rather than denying it.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "demographic_parity_is_error_equality", "pattern": "definition_confusion", "description": "Conflating demographic parity (equal selection rates) with equalized odds (equal error rates)", "trigger": "learner uses one definition's language to satisfy the other", "feedback": "Demographic parity equates selection rates across groups. Equalized odds equates error rates given true labels. These are different measurements answering different ethical questions.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "fairness_is_one_size_fits_all", "pattern": "context_blind_choice", "description": "Picking a single fairness criterion regardless of deployment context or where harm lands", "trigger": "learner applies the same criterion across hiring, criminal risk, and medical triage", "feedback": "Match the criterion to where the concrete harm lands and which kind of error matters most in that context. Different contexts make different criteria the louder requirement.", "rail_action": "practice"}, {"id": "individual_fairness_is_objective", "pattern": "metric_blindness", "description": "Treating individual fairness as objectively defined without recognizing the role of the distance metric", "trigger": "learner cites individual fairness as the value-neutral fair criterion", "feedback": "Individual fairness's content depends on the distance metric d. Choosing d encodes which attributes count as 'similar' and which do not \u2014 itself an ethical choice that should be surfaced.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}